<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taunus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Taunus is a range of wooded uplands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiesbaden">Wiesbaden</a>, bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, Main and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahn">Lahn</a>. The Roman frontier of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limes-germanicus">Limes Germanicus</a> ran along its ridge, with forts such as the Saalburg, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its lands were held by the counts of Nassau, Katzenelnbogen and others. Its highest point is the Großer Feldberg (879 m).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taunus.jpg" alt="Taunus" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: dontworry · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grosser-feldberg-taunus015.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
